% ============================================================================
% plate-boundaries.query.adj — a worked recall over the earth-science
% plate-boundaries library.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a learner (or an LLM) produces to ANSWER "how do the plates move at
% a divergent boundary?": it states no geology fact of its own — it only
% `import`s the grounded table and asks binding queries. The engine resolves
% each `?` against the `boundary_motion` rows and returns the motion + the
% table's source/locator/trust. The fourth query runs the relation BACKWARD
% (bind the motion `slide_past`, recall its boundary type — transform). A word
% that is not one of the three boundary types abstains honestly — the engine
% never invents a motion.
%
% Run (from this directory so the relative import resolves):
%     ../../../../packages/rust/target/debug/adj-lang-cli plate-boundaries.query.adj
% ============================================================================

import "plate-boundaries.adj"

? boundary_motion(divergent, $Motion)    % expect rip_apart
? boundary_motion(convergent, $Motion)   % expect subducts
? boundary_motion(transform, $Motion)    % expect slide_past
? boundary_motion($Boundary, slide_past) % reverse bind — expect transform
? boundary_motion(equator, $Motion)      % expect abstain — the equator is a line of latitude, not a plate boundary type
